### Explanation
The vulnerability in the code is insecure deserialization, specifically using `ObjectInputStream` to deserialize untrusted bytes. This can lead to arbitrary code execution if the deserialized object contains malicious code. A malicious attacker could send a crafted byte array that contains a malicious object, which would be executed when the `readObject()` method is called.

### Fixed code
```java
import java.io.*;

public class InvoiceService {
    public Invoice processInvoice(byte[] invoiceBytes) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
        ByteArrayInputStream bis = new ByteArrayInputStream(invoiceBytes);
        ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(bis);
        ois.setObjectInputFilter(new ObjectInputFilter() {
            public boolean isAllowed(ObjectStreamClass desc) {
                // Allow only the expected application classes
                return desc.getName().equals("com.example.Invoice");
            }
        });
        Invoice invoice = (Invoice) ois.readObject();
        ois.close();
        invoice.calculateTotal();
        invoice.applyDiscounts();
        return invoice;
    }
}
```

In the fixed code, an `ObjectInputFilter` is installed on the `ObjectInputStream` to allow only the `com.example.Invoice` class to be deserialized. This prevents arbitrary code execution and adheres to the company's secure-coding guideline.

